abstract: >
  Reference implementation of an architectural pattern for LLM agent platforms
  in which non-determinism is confined to selection and generation, never to
  sequence, with a restriction-enforcing test suite and successive rounds of
  pre-registered real-model measurement over a persisted external system of
  record. Central measured finding: telemetry blindness -- every
  platform-side observer reports success while the business database holds
  corrupted state -- demonstrated across four model configurations (including
  an open-weights arm with full boundary samples), two system-of-record
  engines (SQLite, PostgreSQL) and two decoding conditions. Two of the
  study's own expectations were corrected by pre-registered measurement:
  id-only selection-format robustness is a model-family property, not a task
  property; and the ambiguity floor in LLM plan selection is attributable to
  the model, not the evaluation set (a paired embedding baseline scores
  27/28 on the same ambiguous intents, narrowing the LLM selection layer's
  measured justification to typed out-of-catalog refusal). A separate
  pre-registered follow-up on the schema+fault cells verified that the
  historical absorbing fallback persists garbage independently of the
  decoding condition; its registered contrary expectation is corrected by a
  dated erratum, since that outcome was determinable from the instrument's
  code before the run. Decoding-level
  structured output suppressed every natural violation measured (bounded
  observations, not elimination claims); the architectural output-contract
  guard is what keeps the channel closed when that mechanism silently
  breaks, at zero measured idle cost and one extra model call per violation.
  Every recorded cost re-derives exactly from token counts and the committed
  pricing table; every absence claim is search- and time-bound; falsified
  predictions are part of the record.
